::ENG4_U04_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Evidence: Seniors could define credit but struggled to compare repayment timelines. Which elaboration is strongest?
{
~A. This proves financial literacy is important because it is important.#Incorrect. This repeats the idea without explaining the evidence.
=B. This evidence shows that students may know terms but still need practice applying those terms to real financial decisions.#Correct. This explains the significance of the evidence.
~C. This evidence means every senior will make poor financial choices.#Incorrect. This overstates what the evidence proves.
~D. This evidence is about school lunch decisions.#Incorrect. This misreads the source.
}
::ENG4_U04_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.R.06] Which paragraph order best develops an argument?
{
=A. Claim, evidence, context, significance, consequence.#Correct. This order guides the reader from position to developed explanation.
~B. Consequence, random detail, claim, unrelated example.#Incorrect. This order lacks logical structure.
~C. Evidence, evidence, evidence, no explanation.#Incorrect. Evidence needs elaboration.
~D. Conclusion, title, question, unrelated fact.#Incorrect. This does not develop an argument.
}
::ENG4_U04_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.R.12] How does a financial literacy argument connect to a broader concern?
{
~A. It only concerns memorizing definitions.#Incorrect. The issue is broader than vocabulary memorization.
~B. It proves students should avoid all financial decisions.#Incorrect. This is unsupported and extreme.
=C. It connects to independence and informed decision-making after graduation.#Correct. This identifies a broader human and civic concern.
~D. It is unrelated to adult life.#Incorrect. Financial decisions are directly connected to adult life.
}
::ENG4_U04_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves elaboration?
{
~A. The evidence is good and my claim is good.#Incorrect. This is vague and repetitive.
~B. The evidence exists in the source.#Incorrect. This does not explain meaning.
~C. The claim should stay exactly the same even if it is unclear.#Incorrect. Revision should improve clarity and depth.
=D. The evidence reveals a gap between knowing financial terms and applying them to real repayment choices.#Correct. This revision explains the evidence's function.
}
::ENG4_U04_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which sentence explains consequence?
{
~A. The survey happened in a district.#Incorrect. This gives context, not consequence.
=B. Without applied practice, students may enter adult financial situations without knowing how to compare real costs.#Correct. This explains a possible result of the evidence.
~C. Credit is a word with six letters.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
~D. Seniors are in twelfth grade.#Incorrect. This fact does not explain consequence.
}
